<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">583–639</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah was an early companion of Muhammad and one of the leading commanders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. He commanded the Arab armies, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-yarmuk">Battle of the Yarmuk</a> in 636, and as governor of Syria received the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> and other cities. He died in the plague of Amwas in 639.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Ubayda was born around 583 into the al-Harith ibn Fihr clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, settled in the lower quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. His full name was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Jarrah. His father, Abd Allah, was among the Quraysh chiefs in the Fijar War against the Hawazin nomads. According to traditional accounts, he was the ninth person to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, embracing it with Uthman ibn Maz'un and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf before Muhammad began preaching from the house of al-Arqam. After the migration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> he was bound in brotherhood to Muhammad ibn Maslama.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Abu Ubayda fought at Badr in 624, where his father fought on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> side; commentators say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> 58:22 was revealed about him after he killed his father there. At Uhud in 625 he stayed beside Muhammad when most of the army fled, and lost two front teeth drawing iron links of Muhammad's helmet or armour from his cheeks. He took part in all the major campaigns, led the Expedition of al-Khabt, and in 630 commanded the Muhajirun and Ansar in the Expedition of Dhat al-Salasil. Muhammad also sent him to instruct a Christian delegation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najran">Najran</a>, calling him trustworthy.</p><p>Traditions place him with Abu Bakr and Umar in the intervention that won the Ansar's allegiance to Abu Bakr at the Saqifa in 632. Al-Baladhuri denies that Abu Bakr sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>; modern historians date his dispatch to Umar's reign, around 636, as commander of reinforcements, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a> then holding supreme command. Khalil Athamina holds that Umar appointed him to the supreme command after the Muslim victory at the Yarmuk. He led the army that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 636 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, then administered them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>During the drought of 638, Umar asked the governors for aid. Abu Ubayda sent caravans, and 4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> loaded with food reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> first; Umar put him in charge of distribution and offered 4,000 dinars, which he refused. He died of the plague of Amwas in 639 and left no descendants. From the 13th century his tomb was traditionally held to be at Ammata in the Jordan Valley, which became known as Ghawr Abi Ubayda, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> and Beisan were also named. The Jarrahs, who claimed descent from him, kept the tomb and were exempt from taxes by the Ottoman authorities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the west, at the Battle of the Yarmuk in August 636 CE, Arab forces under the command of <b>Abu Ubayda</b> and Khalid ibn al-Walid, perhaps the most gifted battlefield commander of the seventh century, defeated a large Byzantine army over six days, in tradition aided by a dust…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in Emmaus Nicopolis, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=619" aria-label="Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5620 1372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 1642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 1642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=619">What was here in 619 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
